<subsection xml:id="course_description" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Course Description</title>
  <p>
    This course is an introduction to graph theory, the study of networks of points joined by lines.
    Students learn the fundamental concepts of the subject <mdash/> degrees, isomorphism, trees, connectivity, traversability, matchings, planarity, coloring, and domination <mdash/> and learn to prove theorems about the structure of graphs.
  </p>
  <p>
    One question runs through the quarter: when can a graph be divided into connected pieces of prescribed sizes?
    Trees and connectivity are developed with this question in view, and the course reaches a complete answer for dividing a graph into two pieces.
    The remaining topics are chosen to show the range of the subject and its applications.
  </p>
</subsection>
